.SH DESCRIPTION
.B weird
scans running processes and highlights suspicious behavior and unclear provenance.
It combines process inventory with package manager ownership, signature evidence,
and behavioral signals to produce a weirdness score and trust label.
The output is deterministic where possible (normalized cmdline, stable sorting)
and can be emitted as JSON/NDJSON for automation.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
Data flow (best effort, never hard-fails):
.TP
.B Collectors
Process inventory: osquery (preferred) or /proc fallback. Network inventory: ss (preferred) or /proc/net fallback.
.TP
.B Signals
Deleted executable, memfd/anonymous exec, suspicious path, random name, bare interpreter, ELF sanity.
A deleted executable whose path is back on disk, owned by a package installed
after the process started, is reported as stale after upgrade (low score) rather
than critical.
.TP
.B Provenance
Package manager ownership + signature evidence (two-axis model).
.TP
.B Policy
Scoring weights and thresholds from config.toml; toggles for higher-noise checks.
.TP
.B Rules
rules.toml allows suppress/downgrade/upgrade/tag with profiles.
.TP
.B Output
Table or JSON/NDJSON; optional baseline/diff.
.TP
.B Partial errors
Collector failures are reported and return exit code 3 when applicable.
.SH DETERMINISM
Process keys are derived from realpath + uid + ppid + normalized cmdline.
Cmdline whitespace is normalized across collectors, and output is sorted to reduce noise.
Network connection lists are sorted in detail output for stability.
.SH PROVENANCE MODEL
Two-axis provenance:
.TP
.B Ownership
Owned / unowned / unknown (package manager ownership only).
.TP
.B Signature
official / third_party / verified / unverified / unknown. On pacman systems
\fBverified\fR means the local database records a pgp check at install time.
.PP
Trust levels are mapped conservatively. APT with official keyring match -> OFFICIAL.
APT signed-by non-distro -> THIRD_PARTY. Other managers only elevate if verification is explicit.
.SH SIGNATURE VERIFICATION
.TP
.B APT
Uses InRelease + keyring fingerprints (strongest support).
.TP
.B RPM (DNF/YUM/Zypper)
Uses rpmkeys/rpm --checksig when cached RPM is available; otherwise reports unavailable.
.TP
.B Pacman
Reads SigLevel policy; per-package verification requires pkg+sig and is not performed.
.TP
.B APK
Uses apk verify for integrity (does not claim signature verification).
.TP
.B XBPS / Portage / swupd
Reports signed-repo/manifest evidence only (no per-package verification).
.TP
.B Nix
Tries nix store verify --no-contents when available; otherwise integrity-only.
.SH BINARY INTEGRITY
The running executable is hashed and compared with what the package
recorded at install time: the sha256digest in the pacman mtree file
(/var/lib/pacman/local/<pkg>/mtree), the md5 in the dpkg md5sums file
(/var/lib/dpkg/info/<pkg>.md5sums, a weak hash but the only one dpkg keeps),
or \fBrpm \-V\fR. The mtree and md5sums files are parsed in-process, so no
root and no package manager call is needed.
.PP
By default only flagged processes are hashed (any score, deleted or memfd
executable). \fB\-\-deep\fR adds every OFFICIAL and THIRD_PARTY process.
\fBinspect\fR always verifies the inspected process and prints an
Integrity line.
.PP
A mismatch adds the \fBintegrity_modified\fR signal (60 points, ATT&CK
T1554) and the verdict becomes "Likely malicious" whatever the trust level.
When the check cannot be made (no mtree, path not listed, unreadable file)
the evidence says "unavailable" and nothing is scored. JSON output has the
result in the \fBintegrity\fR field: intact, modified, unavailable or empty
when the process was not checked.
.SH RULES
rules.toml supports profiles and actions: suppress, downgrade, upgrade, tag.
Run \fBweird rules init\fR to create a template.
.PP
Matchers: \fBname\fR, \fBpath\fR and \fBcmdline\fR take an exact string or a table with
\fBequals\fR, \fBcontains\fR or \fBregex\fR; \fBuid\fR, \fBmanager\fR, \fBpackage\fR and
\fBtrust_level\fR are exact. \fBparent\fR matches when any ancestor comm matches (the ppid
chain is walked up to 6 levels via /proc/PID/stat); \fBdirect_parent\fR matches the
immediate parent only. A chain that ends early (ancestor exited, or unreadable) simply does
not match. All matchers present in a rule must match.
.PP
Optional keys: \fBid\fR names the rule in the reasons list, \fBadd\fR sets the points for
upgrade, \fBattack\fR records an ATT&CK technique id on upgrade, \fBtag\fR labels the row
(on suppress as well as tag). Suppressed processes are hidden unless \fB\-v\fR is given.
.PP
Built-in rules run before rules.toml: builds under makepkg, paru, yay, pikaur or aurutils are
suppressed and tagged "build"; a shell, interpreter, curl or wget whose ancestor is a browser,
PDF viewer, office suite or mail client gains 30 points (T1204.002). Print them with
\fBweird rules show \-\-defaults\fR; \fBdefaults = false\fR in rules.toml disables them.
.SH BASELINE / DIFF
Baseline stores process_key, exe realpath, sha256 (if readable), uid, cmdline (sanitized),
package, trust level, provenance, and evidence snippet. Diff reports new, changed_hash,
moved, and trust_changed.
.PP
\fBbaseline trust\fR \fIPID_OR_PATH\fR records the executable's realpath, sha256,
package and trust level in ~/.config/weird/trusted.json (0600, written atomically,
ignored when world\-writable or owned by another user). A process whose executable
hash matches an entry is suppressed and tagged \fBtrusted\fR (shown with \fB\-v\fR).
A process matching an entry by process key or path whose hash differs is not
suppressed and gets \fBtrusted_binary_changed\fR (+30, T1554) with the date the
trust was recorded. \fBbaseline diff\fR re\-hashes every trusted file on disk and
lists changed ones under changed_hash whether or not they are running.
\fBbaseline untrust\fR \fIPATH\fR removes the entry.
.SH NETWORK ENRICHMENT
Network view uses outbound connections by default. Private/loopback/link-local addresses
can be filtered. \fB--include-listen\fR adds listening sockets. \fB--enrich-net\fR enables
best-effort reverse DNS and simple context hints.
.SH SECURITY & PRIVACY
Cmdline is sanitized by default in JSON/NDJSON and baseline outputs; use \fB--raw-cmdline\fR
to include raw cmdline (may contain secrets). Subprocesses are time-bounded to avoid hangs.
.SH HARDENING
.TP
.B Falco execution
Uses absolute paths (/usr/bin/sudo, /usr/bin/falco) to prevent PATH manipulation.
.TP
.B Cache integrity
Cache is written with 0600 permissions, ownership checks, and a checksum; atomic writes.
.TP
.B Symlink validation
Symlink resolution is validated against allowed base directories to prevent escapes.
.TP
.B Debug logging
Enable with \fB--debug-log FILE\fR or WEIRD_DEBUG_LOG to troubleshoot failures.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B (default)
Scan for suspicious processes. Shows a table and (by default) detail for
high\-score and unknown trust levels.
.TP
.B inspect \fIPID\fR
Show a detailed inspection of a specific process, including open files and a
process tree when available.
.TP
.B trust \fINAME_OR_PATH\fR
Add a process name or path to the user trusted list.
.TP
.B rules show
Print a rules template. With \fB\-\-defaults\fR, print the built-in rules instead.
.TP
.B rules init
Write the rules template to disk. Use \fB\-\-force\fR to overwrite.
.TP
.B cache
Show cache status. Use \fB\-\-rebuild\fR to rebuild file\-\>package mapping.
.TP
.B baseline
Baseline/diff mode. \fBinit\fR writes a baseline, \fBshow\fR prints it, \fBdiff\fR compares
(and re\-hashes trusted files). \fBtrust\fR \fIPID_OR_PATH\fR records an executable hash
in trusted.json so scans stay quiet until the file changes; \fBuntrust\fR \fIPATH\fR
removes it. See BASELINE / DIFF.
.TP
.B restart\-needed
List processes whose executable, or a library mapped in /proc/PID/maps, shows
as (deleted) because a package installed after the process started replaced
the file. Groups them by systemd unit (from /proc/PID/cgroup) and prints the
matching \fBsystemctl restart\fR or \fBsystemctl \-\-user restart\fR line.
needrestart is not in the official Arch repositories; this is the equivalent. \fB\-\-json\fR prints the
same groups. Exit status is 1 when something is stale and 0 otherwise.
.TP
.B persist
List persistence entries and the provenance of what each one launches.
Sources: systemd units in /etc/systemd, /usr/lib/systemd and
~/.config/systemd/user (ExecStart, ExecStartPre, ExecStartPost, ExecReload,
timers followed to their service, drop\-ins), cron (/etc/crontab,
/etc/cron.d, /etc/cron.{hourly,daily,weekly,monthly}, readable spool files,
\fBcrontab \-l\fR), XDG autostart .desktop files, shell rc files (lines
that run an absolute path or source a file no package owns), /etc/ld.so.preload,
udev RUN rules in /etc/udev/rules.d, pacman hooks in /etc/pacman.d/hooks and
/usr/share/libalpm/hooks, and CC, CXX, LD, STRIP, BUILDENV or PACKAGER in
/etc/makepkg.conf and ~/.makepkg.conf pointing at a path no package owns.
.IP
Exec lines are read the way the launcher reads them: systemd prefixes (\-, @,
+, !), \fBenv VAR=x /path\fR, .desktop field codes and %h specifiers. A
shell or interpreter given \-c is an inline script; an interpreter given a
script path reports the script as the target.
.IP
Severity is \fBcritical\fR when the target is owned by no package, its hash
differs from the package, or the entry lives under /tmp, /dev/shm or /var/tmp;
\fBmedium\fR for inline scripts, USER_LOCAL or LOCAL_BUILD targets and shell
rc hits; \fBinfo\fR otherwise. An inline script in a packaged unit or hook
file that still matches its install hash is the distro's own and stays info.
The table lists critical and medium entries and ends with a count line;
\fB\-\-all\fR lists everything, \fB\-\-json\fR prints host, timestamp,
entries (with the MITRE ATT&CK id per source) and counts. Exit status is 2
with any critical entry, 1 with any medium one, 0 otherwise.
.TP
.B hidden
Cross\-view checks for hidden processes, threads and sockets. Pids: the
/proc directory listing against a direct stat of /proc/PID/stat for every pid
from 1 to pid_max; a pid that answers but is not listed is \fBcritical\fR
(T1564.001). Thread ids answer too, so a hit whose Tgid is another pid is a
thread, and every hit is confirmed a second time so process churn is not
reported. Sockets: inodes in /proc/net/{tcp,tcp6,udp,udp6} against
\fBss \-tunapHe\fR (netlink); a socket only one side reports is
\fBcritical\fR (T1014), /proc/net being read before and after ss. Threads:
the Threads: line in status against the /proc/PID/task listing, re\-read to
rule out a race (\fBmedium\fR). Totals: the task count in /proc/loadavg
against the threads counted over the listing, with a margin (\fBlow\fR).
Each row carries a one\-paragraph explanation. \fB\-\-json\fR prints host,
timestamp, self_check, pid_max, listed_pids, hidden_pids, findings and counts.
Exit status is 2 with any critical finding, 1 with any medium one, 0 otherwise.
.IP
\fBweird\fR, \fBweird hidden\fR and \fBweird persist\fR begin with a
self\-check of their own process: a non\-empty /etc/ld.so.preload, LD_PRELOAD or
LD_AUDIT in the environment, or an executable mapping in /proc/self/maps that
no package owns (outside the interpreter's own prefix) prints a warning before
any other output and is listed as self_check in JSON. The run continues.
.TP
.B kernel
Loaded modules compared across /proc/modules, /sys/module (entries with a
coresize or initstate file) and the [name] suffixes of /proc/kallsyms; a
module absent from one view is \fBcritical\fR (T1014). Every loaded module
must exist as a .ko, .ko.zst, .ko.xz or .ko.gz under /usr/lib/modules/RELEASE
and be owned by a package, otherwise \fBcritical\fR "unowned module"; a module
dkms built from a packaged source tree in /usr/src is info. A missing module
directory for the running kernel while another kernel version is installed is
"reboot pending" (info). /proc/sys/kernel/tainted is decoded: unsigned module
(bit 13) is \fBmedium\fR, out\-of\-tree module (bit 12) low, or medium next to
an unowned module, the rest info. With root and bpftool the loaded eBPF
programs are counted and kprobes or tracepoints at getdents or the tcp/udp
seq_show functions are noted. A posture block precedes the findings: Secure
Boot (SecureBoot efivar), lockdown, modules_disabled, sig_enforce,
kptr_restrict, unprivileged_bpf_disabled, yama ptrace_scope, LKRG (active when
/sys/module/lkrg exists or lkrg is in /proc/modules, else not present), the
real\-time sensor (active while /run/weird/events.ndjson was written in the
last 120 seconds, else not running), taint, module counts and the verdict line
"kernel module loading: open / signed only / disabled". Open module loading
without LKRG adds an info finding recommending signed\-module enforcement or
LKRG. \fB\-\-json\fR prints host, timestamp, modules, posture, findings
and counts. Exit status is 2 with any critical finding, 1 with any medium one,
0 otherwise.
.TP
.B self\-check
Print the startup self\-check items and the self integrity result. At every
start weird hashes its own script (sha256) and compares it with the
weirdIntegrity pin in package.json next to bin/ (a pip install carries a copy of both
beside the script); a mismatch prints one red
warning on stderr before any output and sets self_integrity to "mismatch" in
JSON payloads, without changing any exit status. A missing pin is reported as
"integrity pin not found" with no warning. scripts/update\-pin.py records the
current hash after an intended change.
.TP
.B offline \fISYSROOT\fR
Live\-media mode. With the disk under investigation mounted at SYSROOT and its
kernel not running, run the persistence sweep, the integrity of every
package\-owned executable under SYSROOT/usr/bin and SYSROOT/usr/lib (pacman
mtree or dpkg md5sums; a differing hash is \fBcritical\fR, T1554; symlinks and
unowned files are counted, not hashed) and the AUR PKGBUILD checks when a
pacman local database exists there, taking foreign packages from that database
rather than from \fBpacman \-Qm\fR on the running system. No process scan.
\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR hashes only the first N executables. \fB\-\-json\fR
prints host, timestamp, sysroot, a section per part (persist, integrity, aur)
and the combined findings and counts. SYSROOT "/" is refused without
\fB\-\-force\fR (exit 3). Exit status is 2 with any critical finding, 1 with
any medium one, 0 otherwise.
.TP
.B fixture record \fIPID\fR \fIDIR\fR
Capture a live process into a tree that \fB\-\-proc\-root\fR DIR
\fB\-\-sysroot\fR DIR/sysroot replays with the same trust level, score and
reasons: the /proc files weird reads (stat, status, comm, cmdline, maps,
environ, cgroup, exe link target, fd listing, the ancestors' stat and comm),
/proc/stat and pid_max, the /proc/net lines for the process's sockets, a copy
of the executable and the local database entries of the packages owning the
executable and its mapped libraries. Environment values whose key contains
KEY, TOKEN, SECRET, PASSWORD or API are replaced by "redacted".
.TP
.B aur
Supply chain checks on the PKGBUILDs behind foreign packages (\fBpacman \-Qm\fR,
or the local database when pacman is unavailable: validation other than pgp
or an unknown packager). The PKGBUILD is looked up in ~/.cache/paru/clone/NAME,
~/.cache/yay/NAME or \fB\-\-pkgbuild\-dir\fR DIR/NAME. source=() and its
per\-arch variants are parsed (name::url entries, multi\-line arrays, $url style
variables). A source fetched without https is \fBmedium\fR "insecure source",
one on a paste host (pastebin, hastebin, ghostbin, transfer.sh, anonfiles)
\fBcritical\fR. No checksum array or a SKIP for a plain download is \fBlow\fR;
SKIP on a VCS source is info. install= is info (the script runs as root).
When the clone is a git repository the last commit touching PKGBUILD is
compared with %INSTALLDATE%: a newer commit is \fBmedium\fR "PKGBUILD modified
after install" with the diff stat and the pkgver change, uncommitted edits
likewise except a lone pkgver= bump; together with an insecure source in the
same package it is \fBcritical\fR. Foreign packages that still carry a pgp
signature or have no clone are info. \fB\-\-all\fR lists the info entries,
\fB\-\-json\fR prints host, timestamp, packages, findings and counts. Exit
status is 2 with any critical finding, 1 with any medium one, 0 otherwise.
.TP
.B watch
Poll /proc every 2 seconds (\fB\-\-poll\fR \fISECONDS\fR) and score only processes
not seen before, keyed by pid and start time so a reused pid counts as new. Each
new process with a score above zero or a memfd/deleted executable is printed as one
NDJSON line in the \fB\-\-ndjson\fR shape; clean processes are silent and no key is
printed twice. Needs no root and no Falco. One line, "watching /proc every N s", goes
to stderr at startup. Ctrl\-C exits 0. \fB\-\-falco\fR runs Falco in the foreground
with the weird rules instead (requires /usr/bin/falco and sudo).
.IP
\fB\-\-events\fR \fIPATH\fR reads weird\-sensor NDJSON events (schema v1) from
\fIPATH\fR instead of polling; without \fB\-\-poll\fR or \fB\-\-events\fR the file
/run/weird/events.ndjson is used automatically while it was written in the last
120 seconds. Each exec event is read from /proc at once and scored like a scan;
a process gone before /proc could be read is printed only when its exec path was
under /tmp, /dev/shm, /var/tmp or deleted ("short-lived process", T1036.005).
memfd, ptrace and connect events add the memfd_exec, traced and raw_ip_outbound
signals to that pid; module and bpf events print module_load (critical unless the
.ko is owned) and bpf_load (info for a packaged loader, medium otherwise)
findings with "kind", "detail", "severity" and "attack"; a lost event runs a full
scan. The first line must be the sensor's hello; another schema version exits 3.
Paths under /run are followed like tail \-F (rotation and truncation reopen the
file), other paths are read to end of file; \fB\-\-follow\fR and
\fB\-\-no\-follow\fR override that. \fB\-\-journal\fR sends alerts and findings to
the systemd journal.
.TP
.B falco\-rules
Print Falco rules for integration.
.TP
.B man
Show the manual page.
.TP
.B completions bash | fish
Print a completion script for the given shell, generated from the command tree
so it always matches \fB\-\-help\fR. Install with
\fBweird completions bash > ~/.local/share/bash\-completion/completions/weird\fR or
\fBweird completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/weird.fish\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Show all processes, not just suspicious ones.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Include containers, Flatpak, and Snap processes.
.TP
.B \-\-net
Focus on processes with network activity.
.TP
.B \-\-net\-outbound\-only
Only include outbound connections (default).
.TP
.B \-\-include\-listen
Include listening sockets in network view.
.TP
.B \-\-enrich\-net
Enable network enrichment (best effort).
.TP
.B \-\-deep
Hash every OFFICIAL and THIRD_PARTY executable against its package, not only
the flagged ones. Costs well under a second per hundred binaries.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-ndjson
Output newline\-delimited JSON (one process per line).
.TP
.B \-\-journal
Send each alert to the systemd journal: SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER=weird, PRIORITY 2 for
critical and 4 for warning, MESSAGE "weird: VERDICT NAME pid PID score N: first
reason", plus WEIRD_PID, WEIRD_NAME, WEIRD_SCORE, WEIRD_TRUST, WEIRD_ATTACK (comma
separated) and WEIRD_VERDICT. On \fBpersist\fR, \fBhidden\fR, \fBkernel\fR and
\fBaur\fR the non\-info findings are logged with WEIRD_KIND, WEIRD_COMMAND,
WEIRD_SEVERITY and WEIRD_SUBJECT (medium is priority 4, low 5). Uses the
systemd.journal module when installed, else the journald socket, else systemd\-cat,
else logger(1). Read back with \fBjournalctl \-t weird\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-prometheus \fIPATH\fR
Write a node_exporter textfile: weird_alerts{level="critical"|"warning"},
weird_processes_total, weird_scan_timestamp_seconds and weird_scan_duration_seconds
(the subcommands write weird_findings{command=...,severity=...}). Written to a
temporary file in the same directory and renamed into place. The shipped
systemd/weird.service and weird.timer run a daily scan with \fB\-\-journal\fR and
\fB\-\-prometheus\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-log\-file \fIFILE\fR
Append logs to a file (no secrets).
.TP
.B \-\-raw\-cmdline
Include raw cmdline in JSON/NDJSON output (may contain secrets).
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-log \fIFILE\fR
Enable debug logging to FILE for troubleshooting.
.TP
.B \-\-proc\-root \fIDIR\fR
Read process information from DIR instead of /proc (a recorded fixture, or a
copied /proc). Also read from WEIRD_PROC_ROOT.
.TP
.B \-\-sysroot \fIDIR\fR
Prefix for package databases and system configuration (/var/lib/pacman,
/var/lib/dpkg, /etc, /usr/lib/modules and so on). Also read from
WEIRD_SYSROOT. \fBoffline\fR sets it from its SYSROOT argument.
.SH OUTPUT
The table shows PID, weirdness score, trust level, process name, and a short
description. If no description is found the table shows the verdict:
"Unverified" (no provenance, no signals), "Suspicious" (one signal, or any
signals on a process with known provenance) or "Likely malicious" (two or
more signals, or memfd, deleted or masquerade on an unverified process).
Each scoring reason is printed under the row with its MITRE ATT&CK technique.
.SH TRUST LEVELS
Trust levels indicate provenance, not security. Examples:
.TP
.B OFFICIAL
Signed distro repository.
.TP
.B THIRD_PARTY
From APT but signed by a non\-distro key.
.TP
.B UNTRACKED_KEY
Key is trusted but origin is unclear.
.TP
.B USER_LOCAL
User\-installed binary (e.g., /usr/local or ~/.local).
.TP
.B USER_PKG
Installed by a user space package manager (uv, pipx, npm, cargo, rustup, go,
nvm, pip) according to its own metadata under the home directory; evidence
reads "installed by <manager> as <name>@<version>". Go binaries are identified
with \fBgo version \-m\fR. Nothing was signature checked.
.TP
.B LOCAL_BUILD
Built locally via makepkg (AUR or manual build); no repository signature.
.TP
.B APPIMAGE
Portable AppImage (no sandbox).
.TP
.B FLATPAK, SNAP, CONTAINER, KERNEL, USER_TRUSTED, UNKNOWN
Other provenance classifications.
.SH WEIRDNESS LABELS
For UNKNOWN, UNTRACKED_KEY, USER_LOCAL, USER_PKG, LOCAL_BUILD, and APPIMAGE, weirdness labels are
graduated by score:
.TP
.B 0
UNKNOWN (green)
.TP
.B 1\-19
UNKNOWN (A little weird) (yellow)
.TP
.B 20\-39
UNKNOWN (Weird) (magenta)
.TP
.B 40\-59
UNKNOWN (Very weird) (red)
.TP
.B 60+
UNKNOWN (CRITICAL WEIRDNESS) (bold red)
.PP
Trusted processes with nonzero weirdness display "(Maybe Weird)".
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/config.toml
Policy configuration.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/rules.toml
User rules.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/user_trusted.txt
User trusted list.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/cache/file_package_map.json
File\-\>package cache.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/desc_cache.json
Description cache for man/registry lookups.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/baseline.json
Baseline data.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/trusted.json
Trusted executable hashes written by \fBweird baseline trust\fR (0600).
.TP
.B systemd/weird.service, weird.timer, weird\-persist.service, weird\-persist.timer
Shipped user units for a daily scan and persistence check.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No alerts above threshold.
.TP
.B 1
Warning.
.TP
.B 2
Critical.
.TP
.B 3
Internal error / partial failure.
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Trust indicates provenance only. Even OFFICIAL packages can be compromised.
Use weird as part of defense\-in\-depth, not as your only security control.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
weird
Scan for suspicious processes.
.TP
weird \-v
Show all processes.
.TP
weird \-\-net
Focus on networked processes.
.TP
weird inspect 1234
Inspect a specific PID.
.TP
weird rules init
Create a rules file template.
.TP
weird persist
List what starts on its own and where it came from.
.TP
weird hidden
Find pids, threads and sockets the normal listings do not show.
.TP
weird kernel
Loaded module cross\-view, ownership, taint and hardening posture.
.TP
weird aur
Check the PKGBUILDs behind locally built packages.
.TP
weird watch
Print one NDJSON line per new flagged process, polling /proc every 2 seconds.
.TP
weird watch \-\-events /run/weird/events.ndjson
Score every exec the eBPF sensor reports, as it happens.
.TP
weird \-\-journal \-\-prometheus /var/lib/node_exporter/textfile_collector/weird.prom
Scan, log alerts to the journal and write node_exporter metrics.
.TP
weird baseline trust 1234
Record the hash of the executable behind PID 1234; scans stay quiet until the file changes.
.TP
weird man
Show the manual page.
